From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Niklas Sheth <hi@niklassheth.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 12:30:04 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi_m4: release file state behind a per-VM drain
 barrier

A bounded compute job made an unrelated GEM_CREATE on the same DRM file
block for 4.39 seconds behind VM_UNBIND or GEM_CLOSE. Both paths held
FileState while waiting for GPU fences.

Count active drains on each VM under file state before dropping that lock
for a fence wait. Submit to that VM sleeps on the file condition variable;
it drops both state and device admission while sleeping, then re-finds the
queue/VM after reacquiring locks in admission -> state order. Other VMs and
unrelated ioctls can proceed. Overlapping drains retain the barrier until
the last drainer exits, including errors and VM removal.

Re-find VMs by monotonic ID after every unlock. Keep one overall timeout,
and return with state held through bind revalidation and PTE edits. The
barrier bounds the wait to already accepted work instead of chasing new
frames until a healthy VM times out. Preserve close's fatal-signal and
quarantine policy. Empty bind lists still validate the VM handle.

Bind lists may expose earlier entries during a later record's unlocked
wait; document the existing userspace synchronization requirement. No GPU
UAPI changes.

Host tests compile actual drain and submit barrier bodies, covering a
blocked same-VM submit, other-VM/admission progress, overlapping drains,
removal, timeout and fatal-signal cleanup. Device continuous-submit,
concurrent BO unbind/rebind/close and a short gaming check are recorded in
tmp/simplification-review/REVIEW-REQUEST-kernel-bugs.md.

Make the submit-side barrier wait interruptible. A signal returns ERESTARTSYS
before submission state is published, without waiting for the drainer's
35 s bind or 12 s close budget. The drain itself keeps its original policy.
Host coverage exercises a signal already pending and one delivered while
asleep: neither alters the pending fences or barrier, and retry works.
